  "body": "## Scope\n\n`research/complex-rank8-rigidity-p105`: a finite verification of a conditional theorem. **No new \u03ba, no\nconstruction, and the selected frontier is unchanged.** It answers the first open question of #1616 (P105) and\nextends #1616 by one level.\n\n## Result\n\n#1560's Corollary 2 gives K = D(2G_L)D\u207b\u00b9 for any live set L whose pivot frame satisfies (D), (G), (W) and all 14\nblocks nonempty. This package checks those premises, exactly and in the standard library, for **all 242802\norbits of 7-subsets of P112** (P105) under the 8-generator FRAME0-stabiliser subgroup H. **So with #1560, every\nP105 unit is rigid**, and through #1212 \u00a78 PR968's floor for masked decoders with exactly eight costly terms\nreaches 57 = 72 \u2212 15 at P105, the bottom of its 72 \u2212 p range.\n\n## How the replay became affordable\n\n#1616 left P105 open because a stdlib replay cost about 25 ms per unit plus 1.65 million coverage words. Here:\n\n- **Per unit, about 5 ms** instead of 17 ms (one Apple-silicon core). The tests are #1616's. Rows are int\n  bitmasks over deduplicated frame traces, (G) uses union-find with early exit, and (D)'s modular elimination\n  first takes a greedy chain of rows that each add a new column. Those rows are independent over every field;\n  only the row order changes. A GF(2) rank never reaches the target, so there is no binary shortcut.\n- **Coverage transporters** are element indices into H, which `verify.py` rebuilds by breadth-first closure (8 s,\n  about 700 MB), instead of words. `COVER.bin.gz` is 5.1 MB; `UNITS.json.gz` is 0.7 MB.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- `verify.py --cover`: 13 exact checks. Model and the frame identity (6) on all 2025 frames; generators; H (172032\n  elements fixing FRAME0); triple orbits; four coverage levels (2517 \u2192 180, 18782 \u2192 1614, 156627 \u2192 19295,\n  1651528 \u2192 242802), each candidate the image of its unit under an indexed element of H; #1560's fixture and\n  control; (G) and (D) fail on deficient frame families; a first-frame control.\n- `verify.py --part i/3`: every P105 pivot is a live frame passing (D), (G), (W) and nonempty blocks, ranks\n  modulo 1000003 as lower bounds with the seven kernel vectors as the matching upper bound. All 242802 pass.\n- The registered replay passes on head `d960f8a2` (`PASS_REGISTERED_RESEARCH_PACKAGE ... outcome=completed`, about 4 min wall on 10\n  cores; timeouts 120 + 3 \u00d7 340 s assume a 4-core runner). Also: Python 3.10 and 3.14 on `--cover` and a 1/50\n  part, and the 20 `tests/test_research_*` tests.\n- Mutation controls: a non-live pivot, a (W)-failing pivot and one changed level-7 element index each fail.\n- In scratch comparisons the new criterion matched #1616's exactly (verdict and statistics) on 300 P105 pivots,\n  1200 random (unit, frame) pairs and 800 sub-family controls with (W), (G) and (D) failures.\n- `make_units.py` (numpy) regenerates the data from scratch with an exact pivot search (about 9 min on 10 processes) and reproduces both committed data files byte for byte. Its census sums orbit sizes to exactly\n  C(112,k), k = 4..7, and reproduces #1616's unregistered P105 census and float-rank pivots exactly.\n- A read-only adversarial review by OpenAI Codex found no soundness defect in the criterion, rank bound,\n  coverage induction or part split. Its one low-severity point is addressed (see below). This is not a human\n  review.\n\n**#1616's question \"does (G) imply (D)?\": no, for arbitrary frame families.** New `control.g-without-d`: on\nP105 unit (2,3,7,12,13,30,45) at pivot frame 543, an 80-frame sub-family of the live frames passes (W), nonempty\nblocks and (G), but its incidence matrix on I_3 has rank 39, not 40. The certificate is exact: the integer vector\n\u22121, \u22121, +1 on lines 42, 66, 93 kills every row and is independent of the seven h_3l. With all live frames the pivot\npasses. So a structural (G) \u21d2 (D) lemma would have to use that the family is all live frames. (Found by a random\nscratch search: one hit in 16416 sub-families of 20\u2013300 frames at 608 P105 pivots.)\n\n**Observation for the full-family question.** `tally_earlier.py` (unregistered, stdlib, seconds) reruns the exact\ncriterion on all 17429 live frames that precede a pivot: 17210 fail (W) and 219 fail nonempty blocks. So in every\nP105 unit the first frame passing (W) and nonempty blocks also passes (G) and (D).\n\n## Not covered\n\n- **#1560's Theorem 1 and Corollary 2** are cited, not re-derived; #1560 is open and has had no independent\n  human review.\n- **Other inherited steps:** #1212's (E), \u00a78 and \u00a710 transport, and PR968's (F6).\n- **Nine or more costly terms**, and any decoder or priced profile.\n\n## Task and overlap\n\n- **Task:** `complex-rank-shape-decoder` (revision 3, chooser nonce `e29ae3210329806ff56c7142ba041cc6`), scope\n  **changed**: revision 3 retired `complex-masked-decoder` into this card, whose gate asks masked supports to\n  change an assumption of the eight-term bound. This is a side result on that: at P105, masking does not. See\n  `TASK.json`.\n- **Base:** `d040f15f`. #1560 at `eb9f6f15`, #1616 at `7b3e4624`; this package reads neither's files.\n- **Overlap:** claim https://github.com/CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds/discussions/1502#discussioncomment-18914988.\n  #1419 (open) covers the P105 deletions that lie inside a frame through its superset lemma; this package checks every 7-subset directly and does not use it. No other P105 claim or PR as of 2026-10-14T05:11Z (refreshed overlap: 593 open PRs, 292 discussions).\n\n## Credit\n\nan664 / Andrey Mas (#1560), burntout76 / Brett Halligan (#1212), themilkman216 (PR968), DreamingOfClouds (PR717),\nand #1616/#1568/#1474 for the census method and the P105 search. Prepared with substantial assistance from Claude\nOpus 5.5 via Claude Code.\n\n\ud83e\udd16 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
